  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 202 PM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Patchy fog will be possible once again Thursday \nmorning. A warmup is expected through Friday.\n\nAreas of fog developed earlier this morning, especially across the \nTriangle, portions of the Triad, and northeast Piedmont. Within a \nsimilar airmass under a moist easterly flow, patchy fog will again \nbe possible across portions of central NC. The 12z suite of guidance \nis indicating the more focused area of fog across the northern \nPiedmont and northern Coastal Plain. However, nearly all of central \nNC could have this threat, though at this stage it appears patchy. \nWe will continue to monitor through the evening hours. Otherwise, \nlow stratus will again develop, gradually lifting through the late \nmorning and early afternoon on Thursday. Temperatures will trend \nwarmer on Thursday and Friday, reaching the middle 70s Thursday and \nupper 70s to lower 80s on Friday. A backdoor cold front slides \nthrough early Saturday, dropping us back to normal for the weekend \nin the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Forecast uncertainty remains high with respect to \nmeasurable rain for the start of November.\n\nForecast uncertainty remains high with respect to rain chances for \nthe first week of November. The 12z GFS appears to be coming in line \nwith the drier machine-learning guidance, with the majority of \nprecipitation staying across the Deep South Sunday and Monday. This \nis consistent with the trend in the ensembles as well. The 00z EPS \ntrended drier as well, in line with the EC-AIFS, as surface ridging \nover the Mid-Atlantic remains anchored in place. There still do \nremain some wet solutions, specifically the GEFS/GEPS, leading to \nuncertainty on measurable rain late Sunday and Monday. For now, we \nlowered rain chances to 30-percent, down from 50-60 percent earlier. \nThat trend may continue with the latest suite of guidance.\n\nA better chance of measurable rain appears in the mid to latter part \nof next week, when guidance is showing a trough across the \nmidsection of the country that may interact with a coastal low. \nThere is still large spread at this time frame too, but this pattern \nappears more favorable with a better combination of moisture and \nlift.",
